13-10-2013

KEY TO EUROPEAN GENERA OF THE BOLETACEAE WITH TUBULATE OR LAMELLATE HYMENOPHORE
1,0 $ Fruitbodies covered with a true universal veil which disrupts into fragments remaining as floccose-woolly scales on pileus and stipe. Spores almost globose, with a conspicuous reticulate ornamentation distinct even at a low magnification. Spore print almost black.$ Strobilomyces
1,2 $ Universal veil absent. Shape of spore susually elongate, subfusoid, fusoid-ellipsoid or subfusoid-truncate, exceptionally shortly ellipsoid (in Rubinoboletus). Spores smooth or with a fine surface ornamentation detectable only under oil immersion or with electronmicroscopy. Spore print olivaceous, olive-brown, pinkish brown, ferrugineous, brown or reddish brown, but never almost black.

2,3 $ Large part of the stipe surface more or less fertile, composed of a caulohymenium which gradually fragments into small islands of caulohymenial cells as the stipe growsand expands. Spore-bearing caulobasidia present. Species not parasiting on gasteromycetes.
2,0 $ Stipe surface sterile, consisting of a filamentous trichoderm (Šutara 1991:Figs. 1,2; 2005:Fig. 4b). Caulobasidia absent. Species parasiting in a certain period of itslife on gasteromycetes (Scleroderma or Astraeus). $ Pseudoboletus

3,4 $ Spores of boletoid shape, elongate, subfusoid, fusoid-ellipsoid or subfusoid-truncate, usuallywith a more or less distinct suprahilar depression.4
3,0 $ Spores shortly ellipsoid, some with anadaxial applanation $ Rubinoboletus

4,5 $ Hymenophoral trama phylloporoid; lateral strata non-gelatinous with hyphae densely arranged, touching or al most touching each other.
4,6 $ Hymenophoral trama in a fully developed state true boletoid or of a structure intermediate between the boletoid and phyllophoroid types; lateralstrata more or less gelatinous with hyphae loosely arranged, not touching each other.

5,0 $ Hymenophore tubulate. Insufficiently favourable conditions, young fruitbodies form a well differentiated lateral stipe stratum up to 80(–200) µm thick (Šutara 1991:Fig.3). $ Xerocomus
5,0 $ Hymenophore lamellate. Lamellae with abundant anastomoses. Fruitbodies (at least in the only European species of this genus) without a distinctly developed lateral stipe stratum (Šutara 2005:Fig.2f). $ Phylloporus

6,7 $ Stipe ornamentation scabrous, with scabrosities consisting of characteristic fascicles of hyphae ending in elements of a caulohymenium. These fascicles are fragments of a predominantly anticlinally arranged, non gelatinous lateral stipe stratum of the leccinoid type (Šutara 1989:Figs. 1,2).
6,8 $ Stipe ornamentation not as above.

7,0 $ Tubes yellow, becoming olive-yellow with age. Context unchanging when cut. Pileipellis staining violet with NH3 vapours. Stipe base with a smell of iodoform $ Hemileccinum
7,0 $ Tubes mostly whitish or pale greyish, becoming grey,grey-brownish or brown with age. If tubes are yellow or yellowish, then context at first turns pink, reddish or purplish and then more or less darkens to greyish or blackish when cut. Pileipellis not staining violet with NH3 vapours. Neither stipe base nor other parts of fruitbodies with a smell of iodoform. $ Leccinum

8,9 $ Spore print when fresh with a more or less distinct olivaceous tint, e.g. olivaceous,brown-olivaceous or olive-brown.
8,12 $ Spore print without a discernible olivaceous tint, e.g. pinkish brown, lightbrown, cinnamon-brown, ferrugineous or medium brown.

9,0 $ Tube trama relatively thick, with lateral strata in a well-developed state 50–110 µm thick. Cheilocystidia and many ot her cells of the hymenophore contain a bright yellow sap. Hymenophore with a conspicuously bright yellow or golden-yellow colour which persists even in dried fruitbodies. Pileus glutinous or viscid when moist, shiny when dry, with pileipellis consisting of a gelatinous ixotrichoderm. $ Aureoboletus
9,10 $ Tube trama thinner, with lateral strata 25–50(–60) µm thick. Hymenophore with various hues, often yellow but never so conspicuously bright or golden yellow as in the previous case. Pileipellis a trichoderm (often more or less collapsed) or a secondarily changed pellis originating from a trichoderm, only rarely a pronouncedix o trichoderm.

10,0 $ Species saprotrophic, growing on wood of conifers. $ Buchwaldoboletus
10,11 $ Species ectomycorrhizal, not lignicolous.

11,0 $ Pileipellis at the initial stage composed of a palisadoderm. Hymenophoraltrama of a structure intermediate between the boletoid and phylloporoid types. Lateral stipe stratum none or very reduced,  at most 30(–40) µm thick, non-gelatinous. Fruitbodies of a characteristic, xerocomelloid appearance, smaller than in Boletus s.str., with a slender stipe. Pores at maturity angular and relatively large, ca 1–2.5 mm. Spores smooth or longitudinally striate. $ Xerocomellus
11,0 $ Pileipellis composed of a trichoderm, which is sometimes collapsing or passing into an ixotrichoderm or other wise modified. Hymenophoral trama boletoid. Lateral stipe stratum in young or middle-aged fruitbodies mostly well differentiated, often gelatinous, up to 60(–90) µm thick. Fruitbodies mostly robust, fleshy, with a relatively thick stipe. Pores roundish, usually smaller than 1 mm. Spores smooth. $ Boletus
 
12,0 $ Fruitbodies smaller, with a slender and fragile stipe. Tubes short,only 3–6 mm long, adnate or slightly decurrent, ferrugineous, cinnamon-brown,raspberry–to vinaceous red, rarely yellow. Pores at maturity relatively large, ca 1–3 mm. Context of the stipe and basalmycelium vivid yellow. $ Chalciporus
12,13 $ Fruitbodies usually larger, with a more fleshy stipe. Tubes longer than in the previous case, up to 25 mm long, whitish, pale pinkish, pinkish brown, grey-ochreous, olivaceousgrey, grey-brown or sordid brown, never coloured as above. Pores not larger than 1 mm. Context of the stipe and basal mycelium never vivid yellow.

13,0 $ Spore print pale pinkish brown or light brown. Tubes of immature fruitbodies whitish or pale pinkish. Context unchanging when cut. Structure of the hymenophoral trama boletoid. $ Tylopilus
13,0 $ Spore print chocolate-brown or deep red-brown, darker than in the previous case. Tubes of immature fruitbodies grey-ochreous, olivaceous grey or grey-brown. Context staining grey-reddish, green-blue, greyish or black-grey when cut. Structure of the hymenophoral trama intermediate between the boletoid and phylloporoid types. $ Porphyrellus
